        "The drainage architecture and hydrographic network of Rajasthan are uniquely structured by the central Aravalli Mountain Range, which serves as a major continental water divide. The river systems of the state are broadly categorized into three distinct drainage regimes: rivers draining into the Bay of Bengal, rivers draining into the Arabian Sea, and extensive inland drainage river systems that do not reach any ocean but disappear into desert sand deposits or shallow saline depressions. Due to arid and semi-arid climatic conditions, only about one percent of India total water resources are available in Rajasthan, making the scientific management of surface river basins and groundwater reservoirs of supreme strategic importance.",
        "The Bay of Bengal drainage system is dominated by the Chambal River and its extensive tributaries. Originating from the Janapav hills near Mhow in the Vindhyan Range of Madhya Pradesh, the Chambal is the only perennial river flowing through Rajasthan, entering the state near Chaurasigarh in Chittorgarh district. Flowing through Kota, Bundi, Sawai Madhopur, Karauli, and Dholpur, it forms the inter-state boundary with Madhya Pradesh before merging into the Yamuna in Uttar Pradesh. It is renowned for the Chambal Valley Multipurpose Project, featuring four major dams: Gandhi Sagar Dam in Madhya Pradesh, and Rana Pratap Sagar Dam in Rawatbhata, Jawahar Sagar Dam in Kota, and Kota Barrage within Rajasthan. The Banas River, originating from the Khamnor hills in Rajsamand and known as Van Ki Asha (Hope of the Forest), is the largest river flowing entirely within Rajasthan, supporting the massive Bisalpur Dam project in Tonk district that supplies potable water to Jaipur, Ajmer, and Tonk.",
        "The Arabian Sea drainage system comprises the Luni, Mahi, Sabarmati, and West Banas river systems. The Luni River, also historically called Sagarmati and Lavanavati (Salt River), originates from the Nag Pahar in Ajmer. It flows through Nagaur, Jodhpur, Pali, Barmer, and Jalore before discharging into the Rann of Kutch in Gujarat; its water remains sweet up to Balotra in Barmer, turning saline downstream due to high soil salinity. The Mahi River originates from the Amjhero hills in Madhya Pradesh, entering Rajasthan at Banswara and forming a characteristic U-shaped loop across the Chappan Plain before flowing into the Gulf of Khambhat. The Mahi Bajaj Sagar Dam in Banswara is the longest dam in Rajasthan, providing crucial irrigation and hydroelectric power to tribal districts.",
