        "Rajasthan is renowned as a veritable Museum of Minerals in Indian geology, possessing an astonishing diversity and abundance of metallic, non-metallic, industrial, and dimensional stone mineral deposits. The state produces approximately eighty-two different varieties of minerals, of which fifty-seven are commercially exploited on a massive scale. Rajasthan enjoys an absolute monopoly or near-monopoly across India in the production of strategic minerals including Wollastonite, Jasper, Lead-Zinc concentrates, Selenite, Fluorite, Gypsum, Rock Phosphate, Calcite, and dimensional building stones such as Makrana Marble, Sandstone, and Granite.",
        "In the metallic mineral sector, Rajasthan is the leading producer of Lead and Zinc in India, concentrated in the world-famous Zawar mines, Rajpura-Dariba in Rajsamand, and the Rampura-Agucha deposit in Bhilwara, which contains one of the richest open-cast zinc-lead ore bodies on Earth, operated by Hindustan Zinc Limited (HZL). The historic Khetri copper belt in Jhunjhunu district, alongside deposits in Chandmari, Dariba in Alwar, and Kho-Dariba, has been mined since the ancient Harappan and Chalcolithic eras. Iron ore deposits, primarily hematite and magnetite, are mined in Morija-Banol in Jaipur, Neemla-Raisela in Dausa, and Dabla in Sikar.",
        "Among non-metallic and industrial minerals, Rajasthan accounts for over ninety percent of India total production of Rock Phosphate, with massive sedimentary deposits located at Jhamarkotra near Udaipur, which are critical for domestic phosphatic fertilizer manufacturing. Gypsum deposits in Jamsar (Bikaner), Nagaur, and Barmer supply essential raw material for the cement industry and plaster of Paris production. Bentonite and Fuller Earth (Multani Mitti) are extensively quarried in Barmer and Bikaner for oil drilling and clarification industries, while Asbestos, Soapstone (Steatite), and Silica Sand are mined across Udaipur, Dungarpur, and Jaipur.",
