        "Rajasthan boasts an extraordinarily rich and diverse wildlife heritage, spanning arid sand dunes, rocky Aravalli escarpments, dry deciduous forests, and perennial wetlands. To protect its endangered fauna and flora, the state has established an extensive network of protected areas comprising five National Parks, twenty-seven Wildlife Sanctuaries, four Project Tiger Reserves, and several Conservation Reserves. This conservation infrastructure serves as a vital sanctuary for iconic species such as the Royal Bengal Tiger, Indian Leopard, Great Indian Bustard (Godawan), Chinkara, Blackbuck, Sloth Bear, and hundreds of migratory bird species.",
        "Ranthambore National Park in Sawai Madhopur district is world-renowned as one of the premier tiger conservation habitats on Earth. Perched around the historic tenth-century Ranthambore Fort amidst dense Anogeissus pendula (Dhok) forests, it is famous for diurnal tiger sightings and the legendary tigress Machli. Keoladeo National Park in Bharatpur, formerly known as the Bharatpur Bird Sanctuary and designated as a UNESCO World Heritage Site and Ramsar Wetland, is a man-made waterfowl sanctuary hosting over three hundred seventy bird species, historically famous as the wintering ground of the rare Siberian Crane.",
